                <pb facs="0710" n="625" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg625"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.52/492</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Summerlin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-09-04"><hi rendition="#italic">September 4,
                            1919</hi></date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received September
                            22.</hi>]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2349</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Department’s
                    telegraphic instruction No. 2154 of August 14, 1 p.m., relative to the Sonora
                    Agrarian Law, I have the honor to transcribe below, in translation, Foreign
                    Office Note No. 002752 of August 28, 1919 (received to-day): <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>I have had the honor to receive your courteous note No. 1291 of the 18th
                            instant, relative to the representations made by that Embassy,
                            protesting against the Agrarian Law of the State of Sonora.</p>
                        <p>“In stating to you that I have addressed the corresponding authorities
                            for appropriate action, I am pleased to renew, etc.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George T.
                    Summerlin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
